tick: 606
role: model
parts:
- text: "My hypothesis was correct: randomizing the initial configuration allowed the local optimizer to discover a superior solution. The score improved from `2.5726` to `2.5800`, setting a new SOTA.\n\nThis success reveals a key characteristic of the problem's search space: it contains multiple local optima, and the quality of the solution found by a local search algorithm like `SLSQP` is highly dependent on its starting point. A single run, even from a random start, is not guaranteed to find the best possible solution.\n\nA more robust and principled approach is to systematically explore the search space by performing multiple optimization runs from different random starting points and selecting the best result. This technique, known as multi-start optimization, is a standard strategy for global optimization using local solvers.\n\nMy next experiment will implement this multi-start strategy. The script will run the `SLSQP` optimizer in a loop for a set number of trials. Each trial will\
    \ begin with a new random configuration. The script will keep track of the best solution found across all trials and return that as the final result. This method leverages the available computation time more effectively to search for a better global solution, rather than just accepting the first local optimum it finds.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Start Gradient-Based Optimization\"\ntags: \"optimization, scipy, multi-start, random-initialization, exploration\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission improves upon the gradient-based optimization method by implementing a multi-start strategy to mitigate the problem of local optima. Instead of a single run, the SLSQP algorithm is executed multiple times, each beginning from a different random initial configuration. The best valid packing found across all trials is returned as the final solution. This approach systematically explores the solution space to find higher-quality packing configurations.\ncontent: |\n  import\
    \ numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds\n  import time\n\n  def make_constraints(n, epsilon):\n      \"\"\"Helper function to generate the constraint list for the optimizer.\"\"\"\n      constraints = []\n      # 1. Non-overlap constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              def non_overlap(x, i=i, j=j):\n                  centers = x[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n                  radii = x[2*n:]\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j])**2)\n                  return dist_sq - (radii[i] + radii[j])**2 - epsilon\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': non_overlap})\n      # 2. Boundary constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          def inside_x_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_x_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i+1]\
    \ - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          constraints.extend([\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_x_min},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_x_max},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_y_min},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_y_max}\n          ])\n      return constraints\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a circle packing using a multi-start optimization strategy.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      epsilon = 1e-7\n      num_trials = 30 # Number of random initializations to try\n      \n      best_score = -np.inf\n      best_params = None\n      start_time = time.time()\n\n      constraints = make_constraints(n, epsilon)\n      bounds_list = [(0, 1)] * (2*n) + [(0.001, 0.5)] * n\n      bounds = Bounds([b[0] for b in bounds_list], [b[1] for b in bounds_list])\n\n      def objective(x):\n          return -np.sum(x[2*n:])\n\n      for i in range(num_trials):\n          print(f\"--- Starting Trial {i+1}/{num_trials}\
    \ ---\")\n          \n          initial_centers = np.random.rand(n, 2)\n          initial_radii = np.full(n, 0.05)\n          x0 = np.concatenate([initial_centers.flatten(), initial_radii])\n\n          result = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds,\n                            constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 75, 'disp': False})\n\n          current_score = -result.fun\n          print(f\"Trial {i+1} finished. Score: {current_score}, Success: {result.success}\")\n\n          if result.success and current_score > best_score:\n              best_score = current_score\n              best_params = result.x\n              print(f\"!!! New best score found: {best_score} !!!\")\n          \n          elapsed = time.time() - start_time\n          if elapsed > 1100: # 1200s limit with a buffer\n              print(\"Time limit approaching. Halting trials.\")\n              break\n              \n      if best_params is None:\n          print(\"Warning: No successful\
    \ optimization. Returning trivial packing.\")\n          centers = np.array([[0.1, 0.1]]*n)\n          radii = np.full(n, 0.01)\n          return centers, radii\n\n      print(f\"\\nMulti-start optimization complete. Best score: {best_score}\")\n      centers = best_params[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n      radii = best_params[2*n:]\n      return centers, radii\n```"
thinking_content: '**Here''s the plan I''ve developed based on the analysis of my previous submission:**


  I realized that my initial hypothesis about random initialization potentially finding better optima was correct, as evidenced by the improved score from my last experiment. The next logical step, therefore, is to systematically explore the space of random initializations. While "grinding" (i.e., trying many random seeds) is an option, a more principled approach is to implement a *multi-start optimization* strategy.


  Since I can''t run multiple agents, I''ll modify my `construct_packing` function to include a loop. This loop will run the `SLSQP` optimizer multiple times, each with a fresh random initialization. After each run, I''ll compare the resulting score, keeping track of the best solution found so far. I''ve decided on 20 trials with a reduced `maxiter` of 50 to stay within the time constraints and have included time checks. I''ve also refactored the constraint definitions for efficiency. The code will also include a fallback solution in case all optimization runs fail. This systematic search for a better starting point should greatly improve my results. It''s a significant improvement in methodology, moving from finding *a* good solution to systematically searching for a *better* one. This will be reflected in my new abstract and title, emphasizing the "multi-start" optimization approach.

  '
