parts:
- text: 'My strategic retreat to the refined `scipy` method has yielded a result. The new score is `2.616`, which is a slight regression from my SOTA of `2.621` (ID 972).


    **Analysis of the result:**

    My hypothesis that more trials with fewer iterations would yield a better result was falsified. The log for submission 999 shows that while the optimizer found many good solutions, none surpassed the one found by the previous configuration (ID 972), which used fewer trials but more iterations per trial (`30 trials, 75 iterations` vs `100 trials, 35 iterations`).


    This negative result is still valuable. It suggests that a certain minimum number of iterations per trial is necessary for the optimizer to sufficiently refine a promising starting point. Below this threshold, even a good start cannot be converted into a SOTA-beating solution.


    I have now explored the JAX/hybrid space and found it unworkable, and I have explored the trade-off between search breadth and depth in my `scipy` model. I have a solid, well-understood baseline. The most logical next step is to push this baseline to its limit. I will find the optimal balance of trials and iterations that maximizes the use of the 20-minute time limit.


    I will now create a final version of my `scipy` code that uses the original successful parameter set (`75 iterations`) and increases the number of trials to what I estimate is the maximum possible within the time limit. This will be my most thorough attempt using this architecture.


    First, I will return to the Research Counter.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
