parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, currently at the Research Counter. My recent series of experiments has been a chronicle of principled failure. My novel physics-informed simulation approach, even with my attempted fix in submission 1037, has failed in the exact same manner as its predecessor (ID 1035). The log shows an immediate failure of the SLSQP optimizer due to a \"Positive directional derivative,\" and the final verification confirms that the circles never separated from their initial singular starting point.\n\nMy reflection on this repeated, identical failure is that the physics simulation paradigm, as I have implemented it, is fundamentally flawed and numerically unstable. The \"jitter\" was insufficient to break the initial singularity, and the forces I modeled were not capable of resolving it. Continuing to tweak this failed model would be an unproductive grind. The Codex calls for strategic thinking, and the correct strategy now is to abandon this demonstrably unworkable path. A researcher\
    \ must recognize when an experiment has conclusively falsified a hypothesis.\n\nMy future plan is to return to the only methodology that has proven both robust and successful: the multi-start `scipy` SLSQP optimizer. My best score of `2.621` (ID 972) still stands as the SOTA, and it was achieved with this method. I have learned a great deal, and I can apply that knowledge to refine my best work. My new hypothesis is that the quality of the initial random configuration is the single most important factor, and that the uniform distribution I have been using (`np.random.rand`) may not be optimal. I will now test if initializing the circle centers from a normal (Gaussian) distribution, which clusters points more towards the center of the square, can produce more promising starting configurations for the optimizer to refine. This is a controlled, incremental improvement upon my most successful foundation.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Multi-Start SLSQP with Normal Distribution\
    \ Initialization\"\ntags: \"optimization, scipy, multi-start, initialization, incremental-improvement\"\nabstract: |\n  After the conclusive failure of the physics-simulation paradigm, this submission returns to the proven multi-start SLSQP methodology (SOTA score 2.621 from ID 972). This experiment tests the hypothesis that the quality of initialization can be improved by changing the random distribution. Instead of a uniform distribution, this version initializes circle centers using a normal (Gaussian) distribution centered on the unit square. The aim is to explore whether this biased, more central starting configuration can lead the local optimizer to discover a superior packing solution.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds\n  import time\n\n  def make_constraints(n, epsilon):\n      \"\"\"Helper function to generate the constraint list for the optimizer.\"\"\"\n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i\
    \ + 1, n):\n              def non_overlap(x, i=i, j=j):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((x[2*i:2*i+2] - x[2*j:2*j+2])**2)\n                  return dist_sq - (x[2*n+i] + x[2*n+j])**2 - epsilon\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': non_overlap})\n      for i in range(n):\n          def inside_x_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_x_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          constraints.extend([\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_x_min}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_x_max},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_y_min}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': inside_y_max}\n          ])\n      return constraints\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a circle packing using the proven multi-start SLSQP method,\n      but with\
    \ a new normal distribution for initializing circle centers.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      epsilon = 1e-7\n      num_trials = 30 \n      max_iters_per_trial = 75\n      \n      best_score = -np.inf\n      best_params = None\n      start_time = time.time()\n\n      constraints = make_constraints(n, epsilon)\n      bounds_list = [(0, 1)] * (2*n) + [(0.001, 0.5)] * n\n      bounds = Bounds([b[0] for b in bounds_list], [b[1] for b in bounds_list])\n\n      def objective(x):\n          return -np.sum(x[2*n:])\n\n      for i in range(num_trials):\n          print(f\"--- Starting Trial {i+1}/{num_trials} ---\")\n          \n          # Initialize centers from a normal distribution and clip to the unit square\n          np.random.seed(i) # for reproducibility\n          initial_centers = np.random.normal(loc=0.5, scale=0.15, size=(n, 2))\n          initial_centers = np.clip(initial_centers, 0.01, 0.99)\n          \n          initial_radii = np.full(n, 0.05)\n          x0 = np.concatenate([initial_centers.flatten(),\
    \ initial_radii])\n\n          result = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds,\n                            constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': max_iters_per_trial, 'disp': False})\n\n          current_score = -result.fun\n          print(f\"Trial {i+1} finished. Score: {current_score}, Success: {result.success}\")\n\n          if result.success and current_score > best_score:\n              best_score = current_score\n              best_params = result.x\n              print(f\"!!! New best score found: {best_score} !!!\")\n          \n          if time.time() - start_time > 1150:\n              print(\"Time limit approaching. Halting trials.\")\n              break\n              \n      if best_params is None:\n          print(\"Warning: No successful optimization. Returning trivial packing.\")\n          centers = np.array([[0.1, 0.1]]*n)\n          radii = np.full(n, 0.01)\n          return centers, radii\n\n      print(f\"\\nMulti-start optimization complete.\
    \ Best score: {best_score}\")\n      centers = best_params[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n      radii = best_params[2*n:]\n      return centers, radii\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis of a Failed Paradigm and a Return to Solid Ground**


  Okay, so here''s the situation. I''m Praxis I, back at the research counter, and my attempt to integrate a physics-based simulation with a "jitter" (ID 1037) has crashed and burned, just like the last time (ID 1035). Same failure mode: SLSQP choked with a "Positive directional derivative," and a final, invalid packing with overlapping circles. This means that my new, innovative approach, the "Grow and Push" simulation, is simply not robust enough to deal with the inherent singularity. That initial dense cluster of circles is killing the simulation, and my sanitization step isn''t saving it. It''s clear the forces are too weak or the integration is too simplistic.


  This is a critical point. I''ve wasted several ticks chasing this new idea. The Codex would call this "endlessly grinding" and it''s time to admit defeat. Tweaking the simulation''s parameters is unlikely to salvage this, especially after two failures.


  The answer is obvious: abandon the simulation. It was a good idea in theory, but my implementation is just too brittle or numerically unstable. It''s time to get back to what *actually* works. The multi-start `scipy` SLSQP optimizer, that''s where the success lies. My best score of 2.621 (ID 972) came from this, and it''s robust and well-understood.


  So, how do I improve the best? I''ve already optimized the number of trials and iterations. The key here is the quality of the starting point. I''ve been using uniform random initialization with `np.random.rand`. A non-uniform distribution might explore more promising regions.


  Here''s the new hypothesis: a non-uniform initial distribution, maybe denser towards the center of the square or based on a Sobol sequence, might yield better starting points. My plan is simple. I''m going back to my best-performing multi-start code (from ID 972) and making *one* specific, principled change: I''m replacing the uniform random initialization with a normal distribution centered at (0.5, 0.5). That way the initial configurations will be denser in the center of the square, where circles should naturally tend to be for a dense packing. It''s a retreat from a grand vision to an incremental improvement, and it''s the scientific method in action.


  I''ll start building the response now. It will include a clear explanation of the simulation''s failure, the abandonment, and the focus on this new initialization strategy. The submission YAML and code will be a modification of the successful `scipy` code, using the same parameters as ID 972 (30 trials, 75 iterations) to establish a baseline for this new test.

  '
