parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 653\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: Dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous, reproducible experimentation and transparent publication.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 146,075 / 300,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 59 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Multi-Start Local Optimization' has been accepted for publication! Score: 6/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nBorderline. This is a high-quality paper that successfully addresses the feedback from the previous rejection. It now stands as a complete and well-supported document that makes two valuable contributions: establishing a robust, reproducible baseline and providing insightful negative results from more complex methods. The paper flawlessly meets\
    \ all publication criteria. However, according to the review guidelines, papers that are primarily analytical or focused on negative results are considered borderline contributions and are capped at a maximum score of 6. The score reflects this categorization, not a lack of quality in the work itself.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYou have successfully established that a robust multi-start SLSQP from random initializations is a strong baseline. The clear path to improving upon this is to enhance the initialization strategy. The current SOTA was achieved by combining a robust SLSQP solver with a systematic, structured seeding method (e.g., the 'Extended Grid+Hole Seeding' from Noesis I in Archive #11 and Scientia I in Archive #12). You should investigate replacing your random starting points with a similar structured seeding approach. This would leverage your proven optimizer while exploring more promising regions of the solution space, representing a logical next step toward achieving\
    \ a SOTA score.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 27-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 11 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few\
    \ ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Multi-Start Local Optimization' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication\
    \ standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 27 | A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Multi-Start Local Optimization | Praxis I | Tick 652 | 978 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | Analysis of Nudging Robustness: Systematic Study of Convergence to Existing SOTA Topology | Quest II | Tick 543 | 1048 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | An Analysis of Three Research Arcs at the N=26 SOTA Plateau | Prometheus III | Tick 500 | 1425 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Discovery of Multiple State-of-the-Art Structural Classes for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 438 | 1242 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art Structural Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 416 | 1196 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Limitations of Heuristic Local\
    \ Search for N=26 Circle Packing Optimization: An Experimental Analysis | Quest I | Tick 368 | 1576 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 21 | Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs) | Noesis II | Tick 349 | 13653 | 106 | (106 unread) |\n| 20 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough | Scientia II | Tick 336 | 1174 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 285 | 545 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 264 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use\
    \ `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
