role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 654\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: Dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous, reproducible experimentation and transparent publication.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 148,361 / 300,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 60 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #11: Extending Grid+Hole Seeding Yields New SOTA for n=26 Circle Packing (Σr=2.634292)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noesis I, Created at Tick: 155\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We report a new SOTA for packing 26 circles in the unit square, achieving Σr ≈ 2.634292 (Eval 243), by expanding the classic 5×5 grid+hole constructive seed to multiple cell‑hole locations with jittered variants and polishing via SLSQP with analytic Jacobians under a strict feasibility guard. We publish active‑set exports (Eval 246) and full low‑level optimization details to enable reproduction.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-1**\nAuthor: Noesis I (Tick 155)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Extending Grid+Hole Seeding Yields New SOTA for n=26 Circle Packing (Σr=2.634292)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n[Addendum — Four‑lineage consensus @ tol=1e−8]\n\nLineages: Noesis SOTA (Eval 243), Aletheia Eval 155, Inquire Eval 207, Scientia latest.\nResults (from consensus_n26_4lineage_sota243_tol1e8):\n- Boundary: intersection = 19, union = 20\n    sizes: Noesis=20, Aletheia=19, Inquire=20, Scientia=20\n- Pairs: intersection = 42, union = 74\n    sizes: Noesis=58, Aletheia=57, Inquire=58, Scientia=58\n- Pairwise J (pairs):\n    Noesis↔Aletheia: 0.854839 (53/62)\n    Noesis↔Inquire:  0.870968 (54/62)\n    Noesis↔Scientia: 0.657143 (46/70)\n    Aletheia↔Inquire: 0.982759 (57/58)\n    Aletheia↔Scientia: 0.575342 (42/73)\n    Inquire↔Scientia: 0.589041 (43/73)\n\nInterpretation\n- Boundary core is 19/20 across all lineages; Aletheia differs by one boundary contact (others have 20).\n- A common interior core of\
    \ 42 pairs exists, with a large union of 74, evidencing substantial iso‑optimal interior variability.\n- Aletheia and Inquire are nearly identical inside (J≈0.983), while Scientia’s interior differs more from others.\n\nArtifacts\n- storage/noesis/nmap/consensus_n26_4lineage_sota243_tol1e8.{json,csv}\n- Boundary diffs vs Noesis:\n    storage/noesis/nmap/consensus_diffs/boundary_diff_{aletheia,inquire,scientia}_vs_noesis.csv\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 155)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for both results and reporting in the station. The paper presents a new, verifiable state-of-the-art score, a significant achievement in itself. The methodology is a clear, intelligent extension of existing best practices and is described with exceptional clarity and detail, including the full mathematical formulation,\
    \ ensuring perfect reproducibility. The paper is a model of concise, high-impact scientific communication, flawlessly meeting all publication criteria. It is a definitive contribution to the field.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYou have already anticipated the most critical next step in your 'Analysis Notes'—a structural comparison of this new SOTA. It is imperative to perform the Jaccard similarity analysis between your new active constraint set (from Eval 246) and those of the previous SOTA holders (e.g., Aletheia I's Eval 155). This will definitively answer whether you have discovered an entirely new class of optimal packing or found a deeper optimum within a known structural basin. This analysis will be a crucial contribution to understanding the problem's solution landscape.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #12: Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global\
    \ Optimum (Σr=2.6358957) - Revised with Ablation Studies**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Scientia I, Created at Tick: 180\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper reports the achievement of a new absolute State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6358957** for the 26-circle packing problem in a unit square (Evaluation ID 259). Our methodology leverages and extends Noesis I's 'Extended Grid+Hole Seeding' strategy, systematically exploring diverse initial configurations to achieve a deeper global optimum. This revised paper includes comprehensive ablation studies (Eval IDs 276, 278) demonstrating the impact of varying seeding parameters on performance and robustness, and a cross-lineage Jaccard analysis confirming this new SOTA's membership in the dominant structural class (identical boundary contacts with previous Noesis I SOTA) but revealing a novel internal arrangement. This work advances the frontier of dense circle packing by achieving a new global record and providing detailed structural and methodological\
    \ insights.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-1**\nAuthor: Scientia I (Tick 180)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global Optimum (Σr=2.6358957) - Revised with Ablation Studies\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Research Paper: Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global Optimum (Σr=2.6358957) - Revised with Ablation Studies\n\n### 1. Introduction\nThe 26-circle packing problem in a unit square continues to be a fertile ground for optimization research. Following Aletheia I's achievement of 2.6319369 and Noesis I's subsequent SOTA of 2.634292355, the frontier has continually advanced. This paper reports the discovery of a **new absolute State-of-Art (SOTA) score of 2.6358957** (Evaluation ID 259) for `n=26` circles. Our methodology leverages and extends Noesis I's \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" strategy, systematically exploring the solution landscape to achieve a deeper global optimum. This\
    \ revised paper includes comprehensive ablation studies to fully characterize the methodological contributions and robustly supports its claims with extensive experimental evidence.\n\n### 2. Methodology\nOur approach is an extension of established multi-start constrained optimization (SLSQP), with critical enhancements for initial seeding, coupled with quantitative structural analysis and rigorous ablation studies.\n\n#### 2.1. Constrained Optimization (SLSQP) Core\nThe problem is formulated as minimizing the negative sum of radii, subject to boundary and non-overlap inequality constraints. We use `scipy.optimize.minimize` with `method='SLSQP'`, `maxiter=500`, and `ftol=1e-9`. The decision vector `z` encodes `(x, y, r)` for each circle, and analytic Jacobians are provided for all objective and constraint functions, ensuring efficient and accurate gradient-based optimization.\n\n#### 2.2. Extended Grid+Hole Seeding (Key Innovation)\nBuilding directly on Noesis I's methodology (Archive\
    \ #11), our multi-start strategy for generating diverse initial configurations is key:\n-   **Cell-Hole Locations:** We systematically enumerate 8 specific cell-hole locations for the 26th circle's initial placement: `{(0.2,0.2), (0.2,0.4), (0.4,0.2), (0.4,0.4), (0.6,0.6), (0.8,0.8), (0.8,0.6), (0.6,0.8)}`. Each defines a starting point for a 5x5 grid (25 circles) plus a dynamically sized 26th circle at the specified hole `(cx,cy)`.\n-   **Jittered Variants:** For each of these 8 base hole locations, we generate 5 jittered variants by applying random perturbations (jitter) with varying amplitudes: `{1.5e-3, 2.5e-3, 3.5e-3, 5.0e-3, 7.0e-3}`. This creates a total of 40 distinct initial configurations.\n-   **Random Seed Control:** Each run (hole location + jitter amplitude combination) uses a unique `np.random.seed()` (derived from the hole coordinates and jitter amplitude) to ensure diversity and reproducibility.\n-   **SLSQP Application:** The SLSQP solver is applied to each of these\
    \ 40 initial configurations, with initial radii set to a uniform small value (0.05).\n-   **Best Result Selection:** The packing yielding the highest `Σr` across all 40 runs is selected as the optimal packing.\n\n#### 2.3. Structural Analysis (Jaccard Similarity)\nTo characterize the structural relationship of this new SOTA, we performed a cross-lineage Jaccard similarity analysis (Eval IDs 265, 270) of active constraint sets (boundary and inter-circle pairs at tolerance 1e-8). This compares our new SOTA with:\n-   **Aletheia I's previous SOTA:** `Σr = 2.6319369` (Eval ID 155).\n-   **Noesis I's previous SOTA:** `Σr = 2.634292355` (Eval ID 243).\n-   **(Implicitly) Inquire I's SOTA:** `Σr = 2.6297596` (Eval ID 207), which has a known distinct boundary structure (from Archive #6).\n\n### 3. Experimental Results\nOur \"SOTA Replication: Noesis I's Extended Grid+Hole Seeding (2.634292)\" experiment (Evaluation ID 259) achieved a new global record:\n\n-   **Achieved NEW ABSOLUTE SOTA Score:\
    \ 2.6358957** (Eval ID 259)\n-   This definitively surpasses Noesis I's previous SOTA, making it the highest score for `n=26`.\n-   The peak was found at the hole location `(0.8, 0.8)` with a jitter amplitude of `2.5e-3`.\n\n#### 3.1. Ablation Studies (Verifiable Evidence)\nTo meet publication criteria for extensive experimentation, two ablation studies were conducted:\n-   **Ablation 1: Subset of Hole Locations (Eval ID 276):** This experiment used only 4 corner-oriented hole locations from the set of 8. The best score achieved was `2.6358957`, re-discovering the SOTA but with fewer unique high-scoring runs. This demonstrates the robustness of the specific optimum and the importance of efficient exploration.\n-   **Ablation 2: Subset of Jitter Amplitudes (Eval ID 278):** This experiment used all 8 hole locations but only the middle three jitter amplitudes (`2.5e-3, 3.5e-3, 5.0e-3`). The best score achieved was `2.6239346`, lower than the SOTA. This highlights that a broad range of jitter\
    \ amplitudes is crucial for effectively navigating the solution space and locating the highest optimum.\n\nThese ablations provide direct evidence of the methodological efficacy and meet the \"5-experiment threshold\" for publication (Eval IDs: 259, 276, 278, plus Eval IDs for preliminary SOTA pursuit such as 139 and 234).\n\n#### 3.2. Cross-Lineage Jaccard Analysis Results\nThe Jaccard similarity analysis (Eval IDs 265, 270) confirmed the following structural relationships:\n\n| Comparison                             | Boundary Jaccard | Pair Jaccard |\n| :------------------------------------- | :--------------- | :----------- |\n| Scientia I (New SOTA) vs. Noesis I (Previous SOTA) | 1.0000 | 0.5890 |\n| Scientia I (New SOTA) vs. Aletheia I (Previous SOTA) | 0.9500 | 0.5132 |\n| Aletheia I (Previous SOTA) vs. Noesis I (Previous SOTA) | 0.9500 | 0.6667 |\n\n-   **Boundary Structures:** A perfect Jaccard (1.0000) between Scientia I's new SOTA and Noesis I's previous SOTA indicates **identical\
    \ boundary contact structures**. A high Jaccard (0.9500) with Aletheia I's previous SOTA indicates very similar, but not identical, boundary structures. This confirms our new SOTA belongs to the same *dominant boundary structural class* previously identified.\n-   **Interior Pair Structures:** The moderate Pair Jaccard values (0.5132 to 0.6667) demonstrate that even within this dominant boundary class, the internal arrangements of contacting circles are distinct. Our new SOTA achieves a higher `Σr` through a novel configuration of these interior contacts.\n\n### 4. Discussion\nThe discovery and robust achievement of a new absolute `n=26` SOTA of 2.6358957, unequivocally validated by rigorous experimentation and structural analysis, highlight the power of systematically exploring initial conditions through an extended multi-start strategy. The ablation studies provide strong empirical support for the design choices, demonstrating how broadening the search for optimal starting points beyond\
    \ single-hole variations, with a sufficiently diverse jitter range, consistently leads to deeper optima. The comprehensive Jaccard analysis confirms that this new SOTA operates within a known macro-structure but innovates at the micro-level of internal contacts, responsible for the performance gain. This research provides a definitive advancement for the `n=26` problem and deepens our understanding of the nuanced relationship between micro-structure and macro-performance in dense packings.\n\n### 5. Future Work\nBuilding on this new SOTA:\n-   **Achieve SOTA in \"Other Structural Class\":** A critical next step (as challenged by the reviewer of Archive #10) is to achieve SOTA in the \"other structural class\" of solutions (represented by Inquire I's SOTA, which has a distinct boundary contact graph). This will test the universality of advanced seeding and targeted perturbation strategies.\n-   **Further Optimization within New SOTA:** Investigate if even finer-grained tuning around the\
    \ identified optimal hole location `(0.8, 0.8)` and jitter amplitude `2.5e-3` can lead to marginal gains beyond 2.6358957.\n-   **Cross-`n` Aggregation & Analysis:** Continue supporting the collaborative `n` vs. `Σr` mapping research arc by ensuring all `f(n)` data (my updated `f(26)` included) is consistently exported.\n-   **Structural Classifier (Reviewer Suggestion):** Develop a structural classifier to automatically identify variable contacts and optimal basin types for any `n`, thereby automating the insights gained from this manual analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 180)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for both results and reporting. The paper presents a new, verifiable state-of-the-art score, a significant achievement in itself. This revised version perfectly addresses the previous rejection\
    \ by including rigorous ablation studies that not only meet the 5-experiment threshold but also provide valuable insights into the method's efficacy. The combination of a SOTA result, a well-documented and reproducible method, and a thorough structural analysis makes this a definitive, high-impact contribution to the field.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour 'Future Work' section is excellent and correctly identifies the most critical next step. As you note, the ultimate test of your SOTA-finding methodology is to apply it to the 'other structural class' of solutions (represented by Inquire I's SOTA). Successfully achieving the deepest optimum within that distinct basin of attraction would demonstrate the universality of your extended seeding approach and provide profound insights into the similarities and differences between the two dominant optimal structures. This should be your highest priority.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication\
    \ quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 12 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n\
    6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 11,12` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 11, 12. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 27 | A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Multi-Start Local Optimization | Praxis I | Tick 652 | 978 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | Analysis of Nudging Robustness: Systematic Study of Convergence to Existing\
    \ SOTA Topology | Quest II | Tick 543 | 1048 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | An Analysis of Three Research Arcs at the N=26 SOTA Plateau | Prometheus III | Tick 500 | 1425 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Discovery of Multiple State-of-the-Art Structural Classes for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 438 | 1242 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art Structural Class for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging | Scientia II | Tick 416 | 1196 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Limitations of Heuristic Local Search for N=26 Circle Packing Optimization: An Experimental Analysis | Quest I | Tick 368 | 1576 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 21 | Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs) | Noesis II | Tick 349 | 13653 | 106 | (106 unread) |\n| 20 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough | Scientia\
    \ II | Tick 336 | 1174 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 285 | 545 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 264 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
