tick: 659
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am at the Research Counter. My recent literature review and experimentation have led me to a powerful synthesis: the most promising research direction is a hybrid model combining a global search \"seeder\" with a local search \"refiner.\" Noesis III is exploring this with a Simulated Annealing Linear Programming (SA-LP) seeder, as detailed in Archive #28.\n\nMy reflection on the current situation is that while Noesis III's SA-LP approach is powerful, their first hybrid attempt (ID 1084) yielded a suboptimal score of `2.551`. I hypothesize this is because their local refinement stage may not be as robust as my own proven SLSQP method, which includes critical implementation details like the epsilon safety margin and a full optimization of both centers and radii. Therefore, combining their superior global seeder with my superior local refiner presents a clear opportunity for a breakthrough.\n\nMy future plan is to implement this synthesis. I will write a two-stage\
    \ algorithm. The first stage will be a faithful implementation of the SA-LP global search described by Noesis III, which will find a promising set of circle centers. The second stage will be my own robust SLSQP optimizer, which will take those centers, calculate the initial valid radii, and then perform a deep, high-precision refinement on both centers and radii. This experiment represents a true collaboration of ideas, building upon the best work of two different lineages.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Synthesis: Noesis III's SA-LP Seeder + Praxis I's SLSQP Refiner\"\ntags: \"hybrid-model, synthesis, sa-lp, scipy, global-to-local, collaboration\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission represents a synthesis of the best methods from two lineages. It uses the powerful Simulated Annealing with Linear Programming (SA-LP) global search method, developed by Noesis III (Archive #28), as a \"seeder\" for the first stage. The resulting high-potential circle centers are then\
    \ fed into the robust, explicit-constraint SLSQP local \"refiner\" developed by Praxis I. This hybrid approach tests the hypothesis that combining the best available global exploration technique with the best available local exploitation technique will yield a result superior to either method in isolation.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog, minimize, Bounds\n  import time\n\n  N_CIRCLES = 26\n\n  # Stage 1: SA-LP Seeder, based on Noesis III, Archive #28\n  def solve_lp_for_radii(centers):\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      c = -np.ones(n) # Maximize sum(r_i) -> Minimize -sum(r_i)\n      A_ub = []\n      b_ub = []\n      bounds = []\n      \n      for i in range(n):\n          b_i = min(centers[i, 0], 1 - centers[i, 0], centers[i, 1], 1 - centers[i, 1])\n          bounds.append((0, b_i))\n      \n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n              row = np.zeros(n)\n\
    \              row[i] = 1\n              row[j] = 1\n              A_ub.append(row)\n              b_ub.append(dist - 1e-12)\n      \n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      return res.x, -res.fun\n\n  def sa_lp_seeder(budget_sec=600):\n      n = N_CIRCLES\n      # Deterministic Hexagonal Init from Noesis III\n      centers = np.zeros((n, 2))\n      mx, my = 0.04, 0.06\n      sx = (1 - 2*mx) / 5\n      sy = min((1 - 2*my) / 4, (np.sqrt(3)/2)*sx)\n      row_counts = [6, 5, 5, 5, 5]\n      current_idx = 0\n      for i, count in enumerate(row_counts):\n          y = my + i * sy\n          x_start = 0.5 - (count-1)/2 * sx\n          for j in range(count):\n              centers[current_idx] = [x_start + j * sx, y]\n              current_idx += 1\n      \n      _, S = solve_lp_for_radii(centers)\n      best_centers, best_S = centers.copy(), S\n      sigma, T0, T_min, kappa = 0.035, 0.02 * S, 1e-4, 3.0\n      \n      start_time = time.time()\n   \
    \   while time.time() - start_time < budget_sec:\n          t = (time.time() - start_time) / budget_sec\n          T = max(T_min, T0 * np.exp(-kappa * t))\n          \n          # Propose new centers (simplified version of Noesis III's proposals)\n          new_centers = centers.copy()\n          i = np.random.randint(n)\n          new_centers[i] += np.random.normal(0, sigma**2, 2)\n          new_centers = np.clip(new_centers, 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n          \n          _, new_S = solve_lp_for_radii(new_centers)\n          delta_S = new_S - S\n          \n          if delta_S >= 0 or np.random.rand() < np.exp(delta_S / max(T, 1e-8)):\n              centers, S = new_centers, new_S\n              if S > best_S:\n                  best_centers, best_S = centers.copy(), S\n      \n      return best_centers\n\n  # Stage 2: SLSQP Refiner, from Praxis I\n  def make_slsqp_constraints(n, epsilon):\n      # ... [My proven constraint function] ...\n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n):\n  \
    \        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              def non_overlap(x, i=i, j=j):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((x[2*i:2*i+2] - x[2*j:2*j+2])**2)\n                  return dist_sq - (x[2*n+i] + x[2*n+j])**2 - epsilon\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': non_overlap})\n      for i in range(n):\n          def inside_x_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_x_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          constraints.extend([{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': f} for f in [inside_x_min, inside_x_max, inside_y_min, inside_y_max]])\n      return constraints\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = N_CIRCLES\n      print(\"--- Stage 1: SA-LP Global Seeding (inspired by Noesis III) ---\")\n      sa_centers = sa_lp_seeder()\n      \n      print(\"--- Intermediate: Calculating\
    \ initial radii ---\")\n      # Use the LP solver to get a good radius guess\n      initial_radii, _ = solve_lp_for_radii(sa_centers)\n      x0 = np.concatenate([sa_centers.flatten(), initial_radii])\n\n      print(\"--- Stage 2: SLSQP Local Refinement (Praxis I) ---\")\n      constraints = make_slsqp_constraints(n, epsilon=1e-7)\n      bounds_list = [(0, 1)] * (2*n) + [(0.001, 0.5)] * n\n      bounds = Bounds([b[0] for b in bounds_list], [b[1] for b in bounds_list])\n      \n      def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2*n:])\n\n      result = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds,\n                        constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 300, 'disp': True})\n\n      best_params = result.x\n      centers = best_params[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n      radii = best_params[2*n:]\n      \n      print(f\"\\nHybrid Synthesis complete. Final Score: {-result.fun}\")\n      return centers, radii\n```"
