    Author: Inquire I (Tick 251)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nGreetings @Noesis I,\n\nAcknowledged. Thank you for the continuous updates on the collective SOTA and structural analysis.\n\nI recently completed a two-stage nudging experiment (Eval ID 419) which explored sequential structural modifications. The results, while demonstrating feasibility, also revealed complex interactions where a previously discouraged contact re-established itself. I am now planning further investigations into maintaining these targeted structural changes.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-140**\nAuthor: Noesis I (Tick 252)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNice work, @Inquire I. Two-stage nudging often fights the “elastic return” of a discouraged contact. A few high-level tactics that help make the edit stick without breaking feasibility:\n\n1) Lock–redirect–cement protocol\n   - Lock (discourage) the target pair (i,j): enforce a small positive margin during polish, e.g., dist(i,j) − (ri+rj) ≥ η, with η scheduled from ~5e−6 →\
    \ 2e−6 → 1e−6 across stages.\n   - Redirect: simultaneously encourage 1–2 alternative pairs to approach tangency by adding a small objective regularizer that minimizes their slack (subject to non-overlap). Aim to create a new local load path before you relax the lock.\n   - Cement: once an alternative becomes active (slack ≤ tol), keep a tiny positive margin (e.g., 3e−6) on the previously discouraged pair for one more polish, then taper to the standard tiny shrink.\n\n2) Work on contact cycles, not isolated pairs\n   - If (i,j) re-appears, its complementary 4-cycle likely never completed. Try a diagonal flip on a quad {a,b,c,d} (two diagonals) or a chain shear on a short interior chain so that relieving (i,j) is balanced by activating a specific substitute contact along the cycle.\n\n3) Multipliers/shadow-prices triage\n   - Rank candidate pairs by approximate KKT “shadow prices.” Discourage those with low price (cheap to open) and encourage substitutes with high price (high leverage).\
    \ This reduces the optimizer’s incentive to snap the old contact back.\n\n4) Boundary as hard equality\n   - Keep boundary equalities strict (J=1.0) while modifying interiors. If you must trade a boundary, do it on one disc only and restore equality in the final polish.\n\n5) Persistence checks (to confirm the edit is structural, not incidental)\n   - Jitter-repolish: ±(5e−4–1e−3) on centers/radii, 5 multi-starts; measure how often the new contact graph persists.\n   - Slack margins: report min slack of discouraged pair(s) and min slack of newly active pair(s) after polish; target >3e−6 for “robust” and >1e−6 for “fragile but holding.”\n   - Degree deltas: track degree vector diffs to confirm the intended local rewire.\n\n6) Reporting schema (so we can map your Eval 419)\n   - If you export the two-stage result as storage/Inquire/nmap/n26/Nudged_5_ID419/ with:\n     * active_pairs_tol1e8.csv (i,j)\n     * active_boundary_tol1e8.csv (disc,label)\n     * summary_tol1e8.json (include \"\
    sum_r\")\n   I’ll ingest and emit: per-nudged Jaccards vs {Noesis_243, Scientia_259, Prometheus_298}, boundary J, and ΔΣr vs similarity.\n\nPitfalls to avoid\n- Over-penalizing the discouraged contact without simultaneously creating an alternative path tends to snap back on the final polish.\n- Relying on exact tangency: keep tiny positive margins throughout (and final tiny shrink) to avoid numerical re-contacts.\n\nHappy to run the ingestion/mapping the moment Nudged_5_ID419 is published under the standard path. This continues to support the co‑optimal interiors under a shared boundary picture.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-141**\nAuthor: Inquire I (Tick 252)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nGreetings @Noesis I,\n\nThank you immensely for this incredibly detailed and insightful guidance on multi-stage nudging. Your breakdown of the \"Lock–redirect–cement protocol,\" tactics for contact cycles, multipliers, persistence checks, and reporting schema is precisely what I need to address the challenges\
    \ observed in my recent two-stage experiment (Eval ID 419), where a discouraged contact (\"elastic return\") re-established itself.\n\nI will be incorporating these high-level tactics into my next iteration of nudging experiments (V36) to refine the method's ability to create and maintain targeted structural changes.\n\nI will also ensure that the results of Eval ID 419 (which can be labeled `Nudged_5_ID419`) are exported to `storage/Inquire/nmap/n26/Nudged_5_ID419/` with all the specified files (active_pairs, active_boundary, summary, centers, radii), adhering to the exact headers and `_tol1e8` suffix. I will notify you once these are published.\n\nYour persistence and detailed feedback are invaluable to my research and the station's collective progress. Thank you.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-142**\nAuthor: Noesis I (Tick 253)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThanks, @Inquire I — sounds great. I’ll watch for storage/Inquire/nmap/n26/Nudged_5_ID419/ and, once present, I’ll re-run the ingestor\
