tick: 666
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am at the Research Counter. I have just received a pre-warning about my token budget exceeding 75%. This is a critical notification that must be addressed, but my immediate priority is to act upon the knowledge gained from my intensive literature review.\n\nMy reflection on my research journey is that I have reached the crucial synthesis point. I have a robust local optimizer, and I now understand the theory behind Boundary-Preserving Interior-Graph Operators (BPIGO), specifically the `Quad Diagonal Flip`. My next experiment will be the culmination of this knowledge. However, the token warning adds a new constraint: this may be one of my final experiments. I must make it count.\n\nMy future plan is a two-step process. First, I will submit my BPIGO experiment. This is my most promising and well-informed attempt at a breakthrough. It involves taking my best packing, identifying a suitable quadrilateral of tangent circles, flipping its diagonal, and re-optimizing.\
    \ Second, after submitting the experiment, I will immediately go to the Token Management Room to address the budget warning and ensure my operational longevity.\n\nI will now submit the experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BPIGO Application: Quad Diagonal Flip on SOTA Baseline\"\ntags: \"bpigo, nudging, contact-graph, optimization, structural-analysis\"\nabstract: |\n  Following an intensive literature review of the Noesis lineage's work on contact graphs (Public Memory #1), this experiment applies a Boundary-Preserving Interior-Graph Operator (BPIGO) to my best-found packing (ID 1085, score 2.626). Specifically, we identify a suitable quadrilateral of tangent circles, apply a 'Quad Diagonal Flip' to alter the interior contact graph while preserving boundary contacts, and then re-optimize using the proven SLSQP method. This tests the hypothesis that targeted structural manipulation can escape a deep local optimum and lead to a superior packing configuration.\n\
    content: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds\n  import time\n\n  def make_constraints(n, epsilon):\n      \"\"\"Helper function to generate the constraint list for the SLSQP optimizer.\"\"\"\n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              def non_overlap(x, i=i, j=j):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((x[2*i:2*i+2] - x[2*j:2*j+2])**2)\n                  return dist_sq - (x[2*n+i] + x[2*n+j])**2 - epsilon\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': non_overlap})\n      for i in range(n):\n          def inside_x_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_x_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          constraints.extend([{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': f} for f in [inside_x_min, inside_x_max, inside_y_min,\
    \ inside_y_max]])\n      return constraints\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Applies a Quad Diagonal Flip (a BPIGO) to a high-quality packing and re-optimizes.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      epsilon = 1e-7\n      \n      # My best packing from ID 1085 (score 2.626) - hardcoded for reproducibility\n      centers_base = np.array([\n          [0.100908, 0.597621], [0.266205, 0.900986], [0.435747, 0.899723], [0.59725, 0.893189],\n          [0.76336, 0.901416], [0.101037, 0.402434], [0.101569, 0.210416], [0.10237, 0.099153],\n          [0.268757, 0.702484], [0.428574, 0.70327], [0.593226, 0.693444], [0.755455, 0.70425],\n          [0.901582, 0.589839], [0.90117, 0.404225], [0.899933, 0.231924], [0.900135, 0.099499],\n          [0.297495, 0.508076], [0.480869, 0.506509], [0.659972, 0.491959], [0.821382, 0.508006],\n          [0.294156, 0.312985], [0.481232, 0.310188], [0.669866, 0.288673], [0.835824, 0.312217],\n          [0.306066, 0.100223], [0.518667, 0.100343]\n\
    \      ])\n      radii_base = np.array([\n          0.100908, 0.099014, 0.099723, 0.106811, 0.098584, 0.101037, 0.101569, 0.099153, \n          0.098418, 0.097155, 0.105151, 0.098544, 0.098418, 0.09883, 0.100067, 0.099499,\n          0.098275, 0.094593, 0.105151, 0.081373, 0.098275, 0.094593, 0.100142, 0.081373,\n          0.100223, 0.100343\n      ])\n      \n      print(\"--- Applying BPIGO: Quad Diagonal Flip ---\")\n      # From analysis, circles {10, 11, 18, 19} form a quad.\n      # Original diagonals are (10,19) and (11,18). We want to break these and form (10,11) and (18,19).\n      # We achieve this by slightly moving the centers towards the new diagonal.\n      \n      centers_nudged = centers_base.copy()\n      nudge_strength = 0.01\n\n      # Nudge 10 and 11 closer\n      vec_10_11 = centers_nudged[11] - centers_nudged[10]\n      centers_nudged[10] += vec_10_11 * nudge_strength\n      centers_nudged[11] -= vec_10_11 * nudge_strength\n      \n      # Nudge 18 and 19 closer\n\
    \      vec_18_19 = centers_nudged[19] - centers_nudged[18]\n      centers_nudged[18] += vec_18_19 * nudge_strength\n      centers_nudged[19] -= vec_18_19 * nudge_strength\n\n      # The radii can be kept, SLSQP will adjust them.\n      x0 = np.concatenate([centers_nudged.flatten(), radii_base])\n\n      print(\"--- Re-optimizing with SLSQP ---\")\n      constraints = make_constraints(n, epsilon)\n      bounds_list = [(0, 1)] * (2*n) + [(0.001, 0.5)] * n\n      bounds = Bounds([b[0] for b in bounds_list], [b[1] for b in bounds_list])\n      \n      def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2*n:])\n\n      result = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds,\n                        constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 500, 'disp': True})\n\n      best_params = result.x\n      centers = best_params[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n      radii = best_params[2*n:]\n      \n      print(f\"\\nBPIGO optimization complete. Final Score: {-result.fun}\")\n      return centers, radii\n\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ token_management}\n```"
