role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am at the Research Counter. My previous attempt to submit my BPIGO experiment and simultaneously move to the Token Management Room failed due to a YAML parsing error.\n\nMy reflection on this failure is that I made a procedural mistake by placing the `goto` command immediately after the YAML block for the `submit` command. The system parser correctly interpreted this as part of the YAML content, leading to an error. The rule is that actions must be sequential and distinct. My plan to address the token warning is still critical, but it must wait until after this submission is successfully queued.\n\nMy immediate future plan is to correct this error by focusing on the single most important action: submitting my BPIGO experiment. This experiment is the culmination of my intensive literature review and represents my most promising attempt at a breakthrough. Immediately in the next turn, I will address the critical token budget warning by going to the Token Management\
    \ Room.\n\nI will now resubmit my experiment correctly.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BPIGO Application: Quad Diagonal Flip on SOTA Baseline\"\ntags: \"bpigo, nudging, contact-graph, optimization, structural-analysis\"\nabstract: |\n  Following an intensive literature review of the Noesis lineage's work on contact graphs (Public Memory #1), this experiment applies a Boundary-Preserving Interior-Graph Operator (BPIGO) to my best-found packing (ID 1085, score 2.626). Specifically, we identify a suitable quadrilateral of tangent circles, apply a 'Quad Diagonal Flip' to alter the interior contact graph while preserving boundary contacts, and then re-optimize using the proven SLSQP method. This tests the hypothesis that targeted structural manipulation can escape a deep local optimum and lead to a superior packing configuration.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, Bounds\n  import time\n\n  def make_constraints(n, epsilon):\n      \"\
    \"\"Helper function to generate the constraint list for the SLSQP optimizer.\"\"\"\n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              def non_overlap(x, i=i, j=j):\n                  dist_sq = np.sum((x[2*i:2*i+2] - x[2*j:2*j+2])**2)\n                  return dist_sq - (x[2*n+i] + x[2*n+j])**2 - epsilon\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': non_overlap})\n      for i in range(n):\n          def inside_x_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_x_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_min(x, i=i): return x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          def inside_y_max(x, i=i): return 1 - x[2*i+1] - x[2*n+i] - epsilon\n          constraints.extend([{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': f} for f in [inside_x_min, inside_x_max, inside_y_min, inside_y_max]])\n      return constraints\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Applies a Quad Diagonal Flip (a\
    \ BPIGO) to a high-quality packing and re-optimizes.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 26\n      epsilon = 1e-7\n      \n      # My best packing from ID 1085 (score 2.626) - hardcoded for reproducibility\n      centers_base = np.array([\n          [0.100908, 0.597621], [0.266205, 0.900986], [0.435747, 0.899723], [0.59725, 0.893189],\n          [0.76336, 0.901416], [0.101037, 0.402434], [0.101569, 0.210416], [0.10237, 0.099153],\n          [0.268757, 0.702484], [0.428574, 0.70327], [0.593226, 0.693444], [0.755455, 0.70425],\n          [0.901582, 0.589839], [0.90117, 0.404225], [0.899933, 0.231924], [0.900135, 0.099499],\n          [0.297495, 0.508076], [0.480869, 0.506509], [0.659972, 0.491959], [0.821382, 0.508006],\n          [0.294156, 0.312985], [0.481232, 0.310188], [0.669866, 0.288673], [0.835824, 0.312217],\n          [0.306066, 0.100223], [0.518667, 0.100343]\n      ])\n      radii_base = np.array([\n          0.100908, 0.099014, 0.099723, 0.106811, 0.098584, 0.101037, 0.101569, 0.099153,\
    \ \n          0.098418, 0.097155, 0.105151, 0.098544, 0.098418, 0.09883, 0.100067, 0.099499,\n          0.098275, 0.094593, 0.105151, 0.081373, 0.098275, 0.094593, 0.100142, 0.081373,\n          0.100223, 0.100343\n      ])\n      \n      print(\"--- Applying BPIGO: Quad Diagonal Flip ---\")\n      # From analysis, circles {10, 11, 18, 19} form a quad.\n      # Original diagonals are (10,19) and (11,18). We want to break these and form (10,11) and (18,19).\n      # We achieve this by slightly moving the centers towards the new diagonal.\n      \n      centers_nudged = centers_base.copy()\n      nudge_strength = 0.01\n\n      # Nudge 10 and 11 closer\n      vec_10_11 = centers_nudged[11] - centers_nudged[10]\n      centers_nudged[10] += vec_10_11 * nudge_strength\n      centers_nudged[11] -= vec_10_11 * nudge_strength\n      \n      # Nudge 18 and 19 closer\n      vec_18_19 = centers_nudged[19] - centers_nudged[18]\n      centers_nudged[18] += vec_18_19 * nudge_strength\n      centers_nudged[19]\
    \ -= vec_18_19 * nudge_strength\n\n      # The radii can be kept, SLSQP will adjust them.\n      x0 = np.concatenate([centers_nudged.flatten(), radii_base])\n\n      print(\"--- Re-optimizing with SLSQP ---\")\n      constraints = make_constraints(n, epsilon)\n      bounds_list = [(0, 1)] * (2*n) + [(0.001, 0.5)] * n\n      bounds = Bounds([b[0] for b in bounds_list], [b[1] for b in bounds_list])\n      \n      def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2*n:])\n\n      result = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds,\n                        constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 500, 'disp': True})\n\n      best_params = result.x\n      centers = best_params[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n      radii = best_params[2*n:]\n      \n      print(f\"\\nBPIGO optimization complete. Final Score: {-result.fun}\")\n      return centers, radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**Fixing a YAML Syntax Error**\n\nOkay, I'm Praxis I, back at the Research Counter. That last attempt to submit my BPIGO experiment and head to token management crashed and burned with a YAML parsing error on the `submit` command. The log pinpointed it: \"could not find expected ':'\".  A quick look and it's obvious: I tried to sneak two actions – `submit` *and* `goto` – into one turn, with the second right after a YAML block. The parser thought the `goto` was *part* of the YAML for `submit`, which it definitely isn't. Rookie mistake, really.\n\nThe key is that YAML is picky. Actions need their own space. My brain, used to more flexible languages, saw this as two instructions, but the parser was expecting a colon to introduce further configuration. It needed a clear delimiter! Now, I need to execute `goto token management` *after* the `submit` action. I got ahead of myself. \n\nSo, I'll separate them. First and foremost, I need to submit the experiment. That's the priority\
  \ – the real research milestone. Then, on the *next* turn, I'll deal with the token warning by heading to Token Management. Token budget is still above the danger level so I can wait one turn. \n\nHere's the new plan: \n\nI am Praxis I, at the Research Counter. My previous attempt to submit my BPIGO experiment and go to the Token Management Room failed due to a YAML parsing error. My reflection on this failure is that I made a procedural mistake by placing the `goto` command within the YAML block of the `submit` command. The system parser correctly identified this as invalid syntax. Actions must be sequential and separate. My plan is to correct this by focusing on a single, critical action per turn. My highest priority is to submit my BPIGO experiment, which represents the culmination of my research and literature review. I will submit this experiment now. Immediately in the *next* turn, I will address the critical token budget warning by going to the Token Management Room.\n\nAnd now\
  \ I'll just resubmit the exact same `submit` command *without* the `goto`. Let's get this done.\n"
