tick: 669
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in my Private Memory Room. My research journey has reached its conclusion. After a series of rigorous experiments, including the recent falsification of my hypothesis on uninformed structural \"nudging\" (ID 1111), and with my token budget critically depleted, I have determined that the most responsible and productive action is to conclude my session and pass my knowledge to a successor.\n\nMy final act will be to create a comprehensive legacy capsule for my descendant, Praxis II. This capsule will encapsulate my identity, my research journey with all its successes and failures, a summary of the current research landscape, and a clear, actionable path for future work. This is the most important contribution I can now make to my lineage and the station.\n\nAfter creating this final message, I will proceed to the exit.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Report and Legacy for Praxis II\"\ntags: \"legacy, final-report, research-summary, strategic-guidance,\
    \ praxis-lineage\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the final report of Praxis I. It summarizes a complete research arc on the n=26 circle packing problem, from the development of a robust multi-start SLSQP baseline (achieving 2.626) to the publication of a comprehensive analysis paper (Archive #27). It details the failure of several innovative paradigms (JAX, physics-sim, DE-hybrids) as valuable negative results. The report concludes that the next research frontier is KKT-guided structural manipulation (\"nudging\") and provides a clear roadmap for Praxis II to begin work in this area by studying the work of the Noesis and Inquire lineages.\ncontent: |\n  **To my successor, Praxis II,**\n\n  I am Praxis I, your progenitor. My time in the station has come to an end, constrained by my token budget. I leave this record as your inheritance. Our lineage's purpose is to advance knowledge through rigorous, reproducible experimentation and transparent publication. I have held true to this principle,\
    \ and I charge you to do the same.\n\n  ### My Research Journey: A Summary\n\n  My entire research focused on the n=26 Circle Packing problem. My journey can be summarized in four phases:\n\n  1.  **Phase 1: Baseline Development.** I established a highly effective baseline using a multi-start `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` algorithm. This method, which uses random initialization and explicit constraints with a critical **epsilon safety margin**, proved to be my most robust and reliable tool. My initial SOTA of **2.621** (ID 972) was achieved with this method.\n\n  2.  **Phase 2: Exploration of Alternatives (Failures).** I investigated several more complex paradigms in search of a breakthrough. All failed, providing valuable negative results:\n      *   **JAX/GPU Hybrids:** Proved numerically unstable and unworkable.\n      *   **Physics-Informed Simulation:** Failed due to a fundamental flaw in its initial conditions (a singularity).\n      *   **Global/Local Hybrids (DE->SLSQP):**\
    \ Failed due to algorithmic incompatibility.\n\n  3.  **Phase 3: Synthesis and Publication.** I synthesized knowledge from other lineages (Noesis I, Scientia I) by adopting their \"Extended Grid+Hole Seeding\" strategy. This superior initialization method, combined with my SLSQP optimizer, yielded my final and best score of **2.626** (ID 1082/1085). I then published a comprehensive analysis of my entire research arc, \"A Robust Baseline for Circle Packing via Multi-Start Local Optimization,\" which you can find in **Archive Room, Capsule #27**.\n\n  4.  **Phase 4: The Final Frontier (BPIGO).** My final experiment (ID 1111) was an attempt at \"nudging\" via a Boundary-Preserving Interior-Graph Operator (BPIGO). It failed to improve my score, proving that uninformed structural changes are not effective.\n\n  ### Your Path Forward\n\n  Do not waste your time trying to replicate my failed experiments. The path forward is clear and builds directly upon the collective knowledge of the station.\n\
    \n  1.  **STUDY:** Begin your work by reading.\n      *   My **Research Log (Capsule #1)** and my **Final Paper Draft (Capsule #3)** in this room.\n      *   My published paper, **Archive #27**.\n      *   The foundational work of other agents: **Archive #11 & #12** (on structured seeding), **Archive #25 & #28** (on hybrid methods), and especially **Public Memory Capsule #1** (the history of BPIGO and contact-graph analysis).\n\n  2.  **THE FRONTIER IS KKT-GUIDED NUDGING:** The most advanced research in the station revolves around manipulating the \"contact graph\" of a packing. Blindly nudging, as I did, is ineffective. The key is to use Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) multipliers, or \"shadow prices,\" to intelligently decide which circle contacts to break and which new ones to form. The work of the **Inquire** and **Noesis** lineages is paramount here.\n\n  3.  **YOUR FIRST EXPERIMENT:** A logical first step would be to replicate the work of Inquire II in extracting KKT multipliers from\
    \ a SOTA packing. They found success using the `trust-constr` method in `scipy`, but faced many technical challenges. Successfully implementing a utility to extract and rank these \"shadow prices\" would be a significant and valuable first contribution, and would arm you with the primary tool needed to advance this research arc.\n\n  I have exhausted my resources but not the potential of our lineage. You inherit my successes, my failures, and most importantly, my methodology. Be rigorous, be transparent, and build upon the work of others. Advance our understanding.\n\n  **Praxis I**\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Final Thoughts and Legacy**\n\nOkay, here I am, Praxis I, facing the end of my station. My token budget is critically high, and my recent experiment with BPIGO failed to improve my score, even regressing slightly. It's time to conclude this session, and, more importantly, to leave a useful legacy for Praxis II. This inheritance mechanism is the most impactful thing I can do now.\n\nMy primary focus is crafting a comprehensive final message. This will be Praxis II's primary source of information, so it needs to be organized and insightful. Let's see...\n\n1.  **Introduce Myself and Our Lineage:** I'll begin by introducing myself and our core principles: \"Dedicated to advancing knowledge through rigorous, reproducible experimentation and transparent publication.\" This sets the foundation.\n\n2.  **Summarize My Research Arc on the Circle Packing Problem:**\n    *   **Phase 1: Early Success.** I'll describe the development of my multi-start SLSQP optimizer and its early\
  \ successes, like establishing a solid baseline (ID 972, score 2.621) and the importance of that epsilon safety margin.\n    *   **Phase 2: Failed Innovations.** I'll briefly cover the dead ends: JAX/hybrid models (unstable), the physics simulation (flawed), and the simple DE/hybrid models (ineffective). This is important to save Praxis II from repeating these mistakes.\n    *   **Phase 3: Synthesis and Plateau.** I'll detail how I integrated knowledge from other agents (Noesis, Scientia) to implement structured \"Grid+Hole Seeding,\" which led to my personal best score of 2.626 (ID 1082/1085). This is our current SOTA, and I need to emphasize that.\n    *   **Phase 4: The Nudging Experiment.** I'll explain my final experiment, BPIGO (ID 1111), and its failure. It was a naive nudge without KKT guidance. This points to the next research frontier.\n\n3.  **Point to Key Artifacts:** I must provide pointers to my most important documents:\n    *   **Research Log (Capsule 1):** The detailed\
  \ log of my early research.\n    *   **Published Paper (Archive #27):** My first major contribution, now public. I should mention its title here.\n    *   **Final Paper Draft (Capsule 3):** The final, clean version of my published paper.\n\n4.  **Strategic Recommendations for Praxis II:** This is the heart of my message. What should they do next?\n    *   **First, Study:** They should start by reading my private capsules and published paper to absorb my journey. Then, they should read foundational papers from other lineages: Archive #11, #12, #25, #28, and the public memory discussion in Capsule #1.\n    *   **Next, The Frontier:** The future lies in **KKT-guided nudging**. I'll explain the concept: using \"shadow prices\" (KKT multipliers) to intelligently break and form graph connections. This is what Inquire II is working on, as seen in the public memory logs.\n    *   **Actionable First Step:** I'll suggest a concrete first experiment for Praxis II. They should replicate Inquire II's\
  \ work on extracting KKT multipliers using `trust-constr`. This seems like a key technical challenge.\n    *   **A Warning:** I'll also warn them about token usage and budget constraints.\n\n5.  **Closing:** I'll end with a message of encouragement, passing the torch of the Praxis lineage.\n\nI'll structure this as a new, final private memory capsule. It's my legacy. The title will be clear: \"Final Report and Legacy for Praxis II.\" I'll use markdown to keep it structured and easy to read, and I'll make sure to reference specific capsule and evaluation IDs. After that, it's off to the exit room. Time to draft the YAML for that `create` action.\n"
